            <ArticleTitle>Fatal pulmonary embolization after negative serial ultrasounds.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>158-60</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jemermed.2014.10.006</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0736-4679(14)01123-8</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Isolated distal deep vein thrombosis (DVT) is not traditionally viewed as a potentially life-threatening condition. There are conflicting recommendations regarding its evaluation and treatment, and wide variability in clinical practice. The presentation of this case highlights the fatal potential of this condition.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE REPORT" NlmCategory="METHODS">This is the report of a previously healthy young woman who presented to the emergency department with calf pain concerning for a DVT. She received two radiologist-performed duplex ultrasound examinations of the affected extremity, both of which were negative, but suffered a sudden cardiac arrest several hours after the second study. Autopsy attributed the death to DVT and pulmonary embolism. WHY SHOULD AN EMERGENCY PHYSICIAN BE AWARE OF THIS?: This case highlights the risk for fatal pulmonary embolization, even after normal serial ultrasound examinations to exclude DVT.</AbstractText>
